{Into the fifth round and needing two wins from the last two rounds, I was paired with IM and Petroff expert Bela Toth. No time to prepare, so some psychology needed! I met the Petroff only a few times and had a good score against it. This game was particularly memorable.} 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nf6 3. d4 Nxe4 4. dxe5 (4. Bd3 {is the normal move, after which} Nc6 $5 {is interesting} 5. Bxe4 (5. dxe5 Nc5 6. O-O Nxd3 7. Qxd3 d6 8. Bf4 dxe5 9. Qxd8+ Nxd8 10. Bxe5 {Karpatchev,A (2380) -Toth,B (2377)/Baden OL 0102 2001 (7.2)/1/2-1/2} Bg4 11. Bxc7 Ne6 12. Bg3 Bxf3 13. gxf3 h5) 5... d5 6. Bg5 Qd7 7. Bd3 e4 8. O-O exd3 9. Re1+ Be7 {Hector, L-Nachon Menendez,G/Asturias Preferente op 1997 (1)/0-1} 10. Qxd3 O-O) 4... d5 ({He looked at me to question if I knew the line or whether I'd blundered! I played it to get him out of main line theory, although I didn't know this line very well.} 4... Bc5 5. Be3 (5. Bc4 Nxf2 6. Bxf7+ Kf8 7. Qd5 Nxh1 8. Bh5 (8. Qxc5+ Kxf7 9. Qd5+ {Simagin,V-Khachaturov,A/Moscow Central CC-ch 1959/1-0})) 5... Bxe3 6. fxe3 $11 {Kostic,V (2433)-Lampert,J (2188)/15th OIBM Bad Wiessee GER 2011 (8.26)/1/2-1/2 according to Rybka}) 5. Nbd2 {So my psychological gamble had paid off! Now we're just in a game and, more importantly, out of all his theoretical knowledge.} Nxd2 6. Bxd2 Be7 7. Bd3 O-O (7... c5 8. O-O Nc6 9. c3 Bg4 10. Re1 Qd7 {Vallejo Pons,F (2696)-Perez Garcia,R (2426)/ESP-chT Hon Gp1 2009 (2)/1-0}) 8. O-O c5 9. c3 Bg4 10. h3 Bh5 11. Bf4 Nc6 12. Re1 Qb6 $5 (12... c4 13. Be2 $14 {I spent some time on my reply, analysing through to move 17 and beyond.}) 13. g4 Bg6 14. Bxg6 hxg6 15. Qxd5 Qxb2 16. Rab1 Qxc3 17. Rxb7 ({Although Houdini likes this, I think on reflection that} 17. Re3 {might be better} Qc2 18. Rxb7 Nb4 19. Qe4 Qxe4 20. Rxe4 Nc6 21. Bg5 $14) 17... Nd4 18. Re3 Qc1+ 19. Kg2 Ne6 20. Bg3 Rfd8 21. Qb3 Bf8 $6 (21... Kf8 22. Re1 Qh6 23. h4 Nf4+ 24. Kg1 Ne6 25. Rb1 $14) ({After the game Toth suggested that} 21... Re8 22. Re1 Qh6 {was good for him, as he could play g5 and let the queen emerge but} 23. Rd1 Kf8 24. Rdd7 {looks very good for white}) 22. Re1 Qh6 $4 (22... c4 {would keep him in the game} 23. Rxc1 cxb3 24. Rxb3 g5 $14 {but he just hadn't seen the danger.}) 23. g5 Qh5 24. Re4 Rd4 25. Nxd4 cxd4 26. Rxf7 $1 Re8 (26... Kxf7 27. Rf4+ Kg8 28. Qxe6+ Kh7 29. Rxd4 $18) 27. Rxa7 (27. Rxf8+ $142 $1 Kxf8 28. Rh4 Qxg5 29. Rh8+ Kf7 30. Rxe8 Kxe8 31. Qxe6+ {with a winning position}) 27... Qxg5 28. Ra6 Qe7 29. Rxd4 Qf7 30. Rda4 Bc5 (30... g5 31. Ra8 Re7 32. Rb8 $18) 31. Rxe6 $1 {Simplifying the position to an easy win.} Qxe6 32. Ra8 $1 Kh7 33. Qxe6 Rxe6 34. Kf3 Rb6 35. Ke4 {and won} 1-0

