{Aldo Haik of France became an IM in 1977. My second IM opponent in a row, after Bela Toth. Haik and I, and several others, were on 4/5 and needing to win in this, the last, round to be in the prizes.} 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 d5 4. Nf3 Bg7 5. Bg5 dxc4 {So I played the sharpest line I could think of} 6. e3 (6. e4 {can also lead to some double-edged positions} c5 7. dxc5 (7. Bxc4 cxd4 8. Qxd4 Qxd4 9. Nxd4 Nxe4 10. Nxe4 Bxd4 11. O-O-O Nc6 12. Rhe1 Be6 13. Bxe6 fxe6 14. Be3 Rd8 15. Bxd4 Nxd4 16. Kb1 Rf8 {Halasz,T (2359)-Stanciu,A (2280)/HUN-chT2 Maroczy 1011 2010 (6)/ 0-1}) (7. d5 b5 8. d6 Bb7 9. dxe7 Qxe7 10. e5 Nbd7 11. Qe2 Bxf3 12. exf6 Bxe2 13. fxg7 Bxf1+ 14. Bxe7 Rg8 15. O-O-O Kxe7 16. Rhxf1 Nb6 17. Nxb5 Kf6 18. Rfe1 Kxg7 {Ristic,N (2420)-Horvath,J (2525)/YUG-chT Niksic 1997 (2)/1/2-1/2})) 6... Be6 7. Ne5 (7. Nd2 c5 8. dxc5 Nd5 9. Bxc4 Nxc3 10. bxc3 {Petrosian,T-Savon,V/ URS-ch37 Moscow 1969 (5)/1-0 although black later won a game with the same line} Bxc4 11. Qa4+ {Goldin,A (2611)-Ehlvest,J (2587)/Tampa op 2003 (5)/0-1}) 7... Nd5 8. Nxc4 (8. Bxc4 $2 Nxc3 9. bxc3 Bxc4 10. Nxc4 Qd5 $19) 8... c5 9. Qa4+ (9. Nb5 $142 $5 f6 10. e4 {although Black is still better after} Nc7 {Halldorsson,J (2200)-Oral,T (2550)/Selfoss Milk Masters-B 2nd 2003 (7)/0-1}) 9... Nc6 10. Nxd5 (10. Ne5 O-O 11. Nxc6 bxc6 {has also scored well for Black, e.g. Crouch,C (2388)-Webb,R (2372)/ BCF-chT2 0203 (4NCL) 2002 (1.2)/0-1}) 10... Qxd5 11. Nb6 Qxg5 12. Nxa8 Kd7 $2 (12... O-O $142 $1 13. Nc7 cxd4 14. Nxe6 (14. Qb5 Qf6 15. e4 Bc8 {Mokos,J (2289)-Timoscenko,G (2487)/SVK-chT 0708 2007 (3.3)/ 0-1}) 14... fxe6 $17 {After playing 12....Kd7 I remembered the game G H Bennett - M. Stean 1972, which had appeared in CHESS in 1972! e.g.} 15. f4 Qf5 16. Be2 Qe4 17. Qc4 Qxg2 18. O-O-O Kh8 19. exd4 Nxd4 20. Rhe1 Rxf4 21. Kb1 Bf6 22. h4 b5 23. Qd3 Rf2) 13. Qb5 Kc8 14. Rc1 Bxd4 $1 {approved by Houdini and Rybka} 15. f4 Qd5 ({According to Rybka} 15... Qh4+ $142 16. g3 Qh5 17. exd4 Qf3 18. Qxc5 Rd8 19. Rc3 Qxh1 20. Qxe7 Bd7 21. Kf2 Qd5 22. Qf6 Be6 $17) ({Best may be} 15... Qf6 16. exd4 Qxf4 17. Rxc5 Qh4+ 18. g3 Qe4+ 19. Qe2 Qxd4 20. Rc2) 16. exd4 Qe4+ 17. Be2 c4 $5 ({Rybka is undecided between this and} 17... Kb8 {but after} 18. Rxc5 ({Houdini reckons that best is} 18. Nc7 Kxc7 19. O-O Bd5 20. Rf2 cxd4 21. Qc5 $14) 18... Qxg2 19. Rf1 Nxd4 20. Qd3 Nc6 21. Qf3 Qxf3 22. Bxf3 Kxa8 {I don't think black has much hope of a win}) 18. O-O Nxd4 19. Qc5+ Kb8 20. Bf3 ({Houdini finds} 20. Bxc4 Rc8 21. Nc7 $1 Qe3+ 22. Kh1 Rxc7 23. Qxc7+ Kxc7 24. Bxe6+ Nc6 25. Bxf7 $1) 20... Nxf3+ (20... Qe3+ $142 21. Kh1 Rc8 22. Qxe7 Nxf3 23. Qd6+ Kxa8 24. gxf3 h5) 21. Rxf3 Kxa8 22. Qxe7 Qd4+ 23. Rf2 h5 24. Re1 a6 25. Qc7 Rd8 26. h3 Rd5 27. Re5 Rd7 28. Qc5 Qxc5 29. Rxc5 Rd1+ 30. Kh2 Ra1 31. a3 Ra2 32. Kg3 Ka7 33. f5 $5 {Now White had about 10 minutes to get move 45. I had about 20 minutes.} gxf5 34. Kh4 $6 (34. Rc2 b5 35. Re5 c3 36. Rxc3 Rxb2 37. Rec5 h4+ 38. Kxh4 Rxg2 {gets to a similar ending, but where white has his a-pawn instead of the g-pawn. Slightly better for white.}) 34... b6 35. Re5 c3 36. Ree2 cxb2 37. Rxb2 Rxa3 38. Kxh5 Rg3 39. Rf3 Rg6 40. Rc3 b5 41. Rc6 Kb7 42. Rd6 Rg8 43. h4 Bc4 44. Kh6 Rh8+ 45. Kg5 a5 46. h5 a4 47. g3 $2 (47. Rb1 $142 a3 48. Ra1 a2 49. Rd2 $11) 47... a3 48. Rh2 $2 (48. Rb1 $142 $5 a2 (48... Rg8+ 49. Kf6 $15) 49. Ra1 Rg8+ 50. Kxf5 b4 $17) 48... a2 49. Rd1 b4 50. h6 b3 51. Re1 Kb6 52. h7 Rxh7 $1 53. Rxh7 b2 54. Rh6+ Kc5 55. Rhh1 Bd3 56. g4 b1=Q 57. gxf5 a1=Q 58. Rxb1 Bxb1 59. Re1 Qg7+ 60. Kf4 Qh6+ 61. Kg4 Bxf5+ $1 {and here Haik constructed a neat self-mate to finish the game prettily.} 62. Kf3 Qh3+ 63. Ke2 Qg2+ 64. Kd1 Qc2# {Final Tournament standings: 1st V. Hort 5½/6; 2-10th U. Avner, G. Botterill, R. Emerson, J. Flesch, G. Forintos, B. Kouatly, B. Kurajica, A. Miles, J. Nunn 5; 11-17th B. Rind, M. Pagden, C. Pritchett, D. Lynch, D. Horseman, R. Balinas, J.M. Hodgson 4½. 18th= on 4 included GM Jansa, IM Tatai, B. Toth, A. Haik, R. Bellin, P.Sowray, J. Nicholson, S. Knott, P. Jamieson, C. Cooley, D. Cummings, G. Flear, M. Basman, R. Borngaesser, B. Cafferty [and J. Saunders - ed. Compiled from BCM, Feb 1979, p70 & JS' own contemporary notes]. I think this was, at the time, the strongest Open tournament ever held in the UK. Mentioned in Leonard Barden's Guardian column in typical fashion - "Emerson beat two International Masters in the final two rounds, but this paled into insignificance compared with Julian Hodgson's feat at age 13....!" And having advertised the tournament on its back page for six months, CHESS didn't cover the tournament at all!!} 0-1

